            "text": "Pairs up two sets of points - this frame's and the last frame's, or this frame's and a reference - so that the total distance between partners is as small as possible. That is how identities are kept: the blob found near where performer 2 was is performer 2, even though a detector (scipy.label, scipy.kmeans, a depth camera) lists the people in a different order every frame.\n\nWith track on, the node remembers: each frame is matched against the last, and the points come out in a stable order, one row per identity. Someone who disappears keeps their row (as nan) and their last position, so they are picked up again when they return; someone new is added as a new row.\n\nIt also solves the general problem: give it a cost matrix (input: cost matrix) and it finds the one-to-one pairing of rows with columns with the smallest total cost - assigning performers to roles, lights to targets, sounds to dancers.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.linear_sum_assignment\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThis frame's points, rows of coordinates (or, as a cost matrix, the costs). Every input is matched and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nreference:\nThe points to match against, held. With track on, the first frame becomes the reference and after that each frame's result does.\n\nmax distance (float box, also an inlet):\nPartners further apart than this are not paired - the reference point counts as lost, the new point as new. 0 (the default) pairs everything it can.\n\ntrack:\nAn option, under the node: keep identities from frame to frame, as described above.\n\ninput:\nAn option, under the node: points (the default), or cost matrix - rows to be assigned to columns, the matrix holding the cost of each pairing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmatched:\nFor each reference point (each row of the cost matrix), the position of its partner in this frame, or -1 for none.\n\ntracked:\nThis frame's points in the reference's order: one row per identity, nan for any that were lost, then any new points.\n\ncost:\nThe total distance (or cost) of the pairing. Sent first, then tracked, then matched.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThree points, sent in a different random order twice a second (left: the list keeps changing). With track on, the tracked output (right) is always in the same order - the same point in the same row - because each frame is matched to the last.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.kdtree finds the nearest point to each point, without making the pairs one to one. scipy.label and scipy.kmeans find the points to track. np.random.permutation shuffles."
        },
